From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: a thread woken from waiting for its messages is not hung

wineserver takes a thread for hung when it has not read its messages for
five seconds and is not waiting for them at that moment. A thread that had
waited idle for longer and was just woken by something else -- an X event,
one of its handles -- was therefore "hung" while it handled that, and a
SendMessageTimeout with SMTO_ABORTIFHUNG to it failed at once, the message
dropped. Broadcasts use that flag: a new display scale's WM_SETTINGCHANGE
never reached a notice sitting idle on screen (woken by the taskbar's
redraw just then), and it stayed at the old scale -- since 1126 made
explorer's own broadcast arrive at that moment, every time, for one of two
notices.

The thread's queue counts as read when the thread stops waiting on it: it
was answering until then. A thread that really stops reading its messages
is still hung five seconds later.

Test: test/hungwake-gate.sh (a thread idle 7 s, woken, at work 1.5 s gets
the message); mutant SG_MUTANT_HUNG_AFTER_WAIT (server/queue.c).
sg-shell hidpi-live-check passes again (the notices back at 100%).
---
--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -1253,6 +1253,15 @@
 {
     struct msg_queue *queue = (struct msg_queue *)obj;
 
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_HUNG_AFTER_WAIT
+    /* Stained Glass OS: a thread that was waiting for its messages until now
+     * is not hung (is_queue_hung): it answered as soon as it could. Without
+     * this a thread idle for five seconds and woken by something else -- an
+     * X event, its own handle -- was taken for hung while it did that, and
+     * SMTO_ABORTIFHUNG sends to it were dropped: a new display scale's
+     * WM_SETTINGCHANGE never reached a notice, which stayed at the old scale */
+    if (get_wait_queue_thread( entry )->queue == queue) queue->last_get_msg = current_time;
+#endif
     remove_queue( obj, entry );
     if (queue->fd && list_empty( &obj->wait_queue ))  /* last on the queue is gone */
         set_fd_events( queue->fd, 0 );
